# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NO SECRETS. This repository is PUBLIC.
# The real policy is a mental allowlist: if you can't say why a file is safe,
# it doesn't go in. The lines below are the net, not the policy.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
*.gpg
*.asc
.netrc
.npmrc
.pypirc
**/credentials
**/secrets*
**/*_token*
**/*_secret*
.aws/
.gnupg/
# NOT a blanket `.ssh/`: that rule silently swallowed this repo's own
# ssh/ package, so the generic multiplex fragment was never committed.
# Block the KEYS by name instead — precise beats broad when the broad rule
# also hides something you meant to publish.
# Caught by tests/repo-hygiene.test.sh, which fails if the fragment is untracked.
**/.ssh/id_*
**/.ssh/*_rsa
**/.ssh/*_ed25519
**/.ssh/known_hosts*
**/.ssh/authorized_keys
**/.ssh/cm/

# Local tool state — derived, not configuration
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~
.zcompdump*
.zsh_history
.bash_history
*.log
.rtk-eval/
# Instruments: clones of OTHER repositories, fetched by measure/bootstrap.sh.
# Versioning them would vendor a third party into this one and put its history
# inside our numbers. tests/instruments.test.sh asserts this rule holds.
.instruments/

# Your real allowlist — derived from YOUR sessions, never versioned.
# The .example is the template; see .claude/settings.json.example
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/settings.json
# Never version a user-level settings.json: Claude Code rewrites it at runtime
# via /config and /permissions, so a tracked copy fights the tool. The env vars
# it would hold live in zsh/.zshrc instead. See docs/claude-code-tuning.md
claude/.claude/settings.json
claude/.claude/settings.local.json
